WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.160
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Frumpies from
Chicago, from BESE.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.410
You're on the air.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:12.900
From there, you're on the air, how are you?

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:21.470
Speaker 3: Tonight, thank you so I saw some
other countries are testing out a four day

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:22.470
workweek, have you heard of that?

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.910
Speaker 1: Yep, we covered that on the bonus
show last week.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:32.840
Speaker 3: OK, so I'm thinking that some states
in the US should try this, and I've seen some

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:39.350
justifications stating things like, well,
the five day workweek was based on one family

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.290
member and the typical household staying home
and taking care of things, one person being

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.290
the breadwinner.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:49.250
So I was just wondering what you were thinking
of the justifications to move to a four day

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.210
work week?

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.280
I've heard of the productivity, but I just
thought to get your comments on.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.280
Yeah.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.399
Speaker 1: What do you what do you when you
say states should try it, do you mean states

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:01.530
should mandate that businesses only have people
work for days?

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.760
You mean like state employees should do it?

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:11.320
Speaker 3: That's a good distinction, I was
thinking it was just a labor law within a

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.479
certain state.

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:14.600
Speaker 1: Yeah, that's never going to happen.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.610
But let's let's put that aside.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.990
I mean, it's not going to happen anytime soon.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:17.990
Let's put that aside.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:22.150
Here's why I think for four day a week is
very interesting.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:27.990
There's a real difference between having two
days off versus three days off a week.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.860
And when you have the three days off and you've
got that middle day where you were off yesterday

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.159
and you're off tomorrow, I think that psychologically
there's a massive benefit to that.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:43.110
And the benefit to that is that then people
are more productive on the four days that

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:47.420
they are working, even if in total they're
working slightly fewer hours.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:52.680
We've sort of adopted this at The David Pakman
Show and that we film our five shows each

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.290
week in four days.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:00.490
So like we're not off on Friday, but it's
sort of like a catch up administrative work.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:04.649
You know, everybody has the stuff we work
on on Fridays, but like the core filming is

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:06.229
all happening four days a week.

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:11.209
And I know for me psychologically, when we're
done on Thursday, it's like, oh, I'm not doing

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:14.629
like a core filming day Friday, Saturday or
Sunday.

00:02:14.629 --> 00:02:15.629
That's really great.

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:18.809
And I think it makes me significantly more
productive Monday, Monday through Thursday.

00:02:18.809 --> 00:02:26.120
If I had child care considerations, I think
it would be significantly more convenient

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.239
and also cheaper from the standpoint of child
care.

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:33.879
If I have three days and then my significant
other has three days, I think that there's

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:35.510
significant benefits there.

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:39.040
But yeah, I don't think there's anything magical
about a five day a week work week.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.309
And the study that we looked at last week
on the bonus show was very positive as far

00:02:44.309 --> 00:02:45.309
as that goes.

00:02:45.309 --> 00:02:46.480
Speaker 3: Mm hmm.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.569
OK, well, God knows I could use another day.

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:49.959
Thanks for taking my call.

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:50.959
All right.

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:51.959
Producer that's doing OK.

00:02:51.959 --> 00:02:52.959
Talk to you later.

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:53.959
Speaker 1: All right.

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:54.959
My pleasure.

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:54.963
Thank you very much.

